  "_design": "Four shelves down rather than up, so this is the route where the LEDGES are the point: every bank on it is a shortcut back the way you came and a wall in the direction you are going, and the three passes are a long way apart on purpose. The ground changes on every shelf and never changes back -- moor, then thin turf with the stone coming through, then salt grass and the pans, then sand -- so the descent is legible without one line of text.",
  "_plan": "The sea has to be SEEN before it can be REACHED, and the map is built round that one requirement. There is no water at all on Gorsehead. The headland on the Wind Stair is a dead-end spur that exists solely so the player walks out to the end of it, sees the whole Caeloran Sea eleven rows below, and cannot get down: the sign says so, and the sea-mark stone standing there says the same thing in the other direction, because vessels line it up with the beacon the player lit their way past on the ridge. Then two more cliff bands and the sea arrives, in pieces -- a bite of deep water beside the pans, then the channels on the strand. The far pocket of the cove is behind a channel and stays shut until the Tide Hall.",
